freeglut — standalone "glutshapes" library (geometry + fonts, no windowing)
===========================================================================

This is NOT a freeglut backend. A freeglut backend (x11, cocoa, osmesa, ogc, …)
is a platform that implements the windowing/event/context contract — it provides
glutCreateWindow, glutMainLoop, input handling, and a GL context. "glutshapes"
provides NONE of that. It is a small library containing only freeglut's drawing
code — the glutSolid*/glutWire* solids, the teapot/teacup/teaspoon, and the
stroke/bitmap text (glutStrokeString/glutBitmapString) — with no window system,
event loop, or context creation at all.

It is meant for environments that ALREADY emulate the GLUT windowing API and
supply their own GL context, and just want freeglut's shapes and fonts on top:
notably Emscripten (whose library_glut.js implements glutCreateWindow/
glutMainLoop against an HTML canvas), optionally with gl4es to provide the legacy
GL entry points the shapes use on top of WebGL/GLES.

Because the host already implements the GLUT windowing API, a freeglut "null
windowing backend" would be the wrong tool: it would still export
glutCreateWindow/glutMainLoop and collide with the host's own GLUT. glutshapes
ships only the drawing functions, so it composes cleanly with a host GLUT.


Build
-----

    cmake -DFREEGLUT_BUILD_GLUTSHAPES=ON -B build .
    cmake --build build --target glutshapes

This produces the static library `glutshapes`. The option is independent of the
platform-selection chain (it pulls in no backend and links no window-system
libraries), so it can be built in any configuration.


Symbols and headers
-------------------

The shape and font entry points keep their normal glut* names (glutSolidTeapot,
glutStrokeString, glutSetVertexAttribCoord3, …) — that is the product you link
against. The handful of freeglut-internal support symbols the geometry needs
(fgState, fgStructure, fgError, fgWarning, glutGetProcAddress) are #define-renamed
to private fgshapes_* names, so the library is self-contained and never collides
with — or silently binds to — a host that already implements the GLUT/GL support
API.

A consumer includes <GL/freeglut.h> for the shape/font prototypes and, for the
few standalone hooks, <GL/glutshapes.h>:

    void glutShapesSetProcAddressFunc(void *(*resolver)(const char *name));
    void glutShapesSetGLVersion(int major);
    void glutShapesInit(void);


Draw paths and the GLES2/WebGL caveat
-------------------------------------

  - By default the GL 1.1 fixed-function path is used (client vertex arrays for
    the solids; glBitmap / immediate-mode glBegin for the fonts). This works on
    desktop GL, on the OSMesa backend (README.osmesa), and on **gl4es** (which
    emulates these legacy calls on top of GLES) — gl4es is therefore the natural
    companion for using glutshapes in the browser.

  - On a raw GLES2/WebGL context (no gl4es) only the GL 2.0 vertex-attrib path is
    valid: build with -DFREEGLUT_GLES, call glutShapesSetGLVersion(2) (or
    glutShapesInit after installing a proc-address resolver), bind a shader, and
    pass the attribute locations via glutSetVertexAttribCoord3/Normal/TexCoord2.
    The fixed-function fonts require gl4es in that case.


Verification
------------

When OSMesa is also enabled (-DFREEGLUT_OSMESA=ON -DFREEGLUT_BUILD_GLUTSHAPES=ON),
a functional test `glutshapes_selftest` is built that drives glutshapes with a
raw OSMesa context and no freeglut windowing linked, confirming the solids and
stroke fonts render standalone (sphere/teapot polygon-token capture, stroke-font
line-token capture, and a non-blank framebuffer).
